    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • Chinese Brand AITO Plans 2027 European Launch\nRenault Issues Fire Risk Recall for Captur and Symbioz Hybrid Vehicles\n8 photos\nPhoto: Renault\nA small number of Captur and Symbioz crossovers are being recalled in Europe because of a potentially compromised busbar-to-relay connection inside the traction battery. A bad joint in the high-voltage battery of a hybrid vehicle is not the kind of thing you or even Renault ignore.\nPer the European Commission's Safety Gate rapid alert system for dangerous non-food products, the recall covers Captur and Symbioz hybrids produced between December 1, 2025, and February 6, 2026. The French automaker uses 0FDZ to identify said campaign.\nThe vehicles in question are based on the CMF-B platform, with both the Captur and Symbioz hailing from the Valladolid plant in Spain. Although they are identical in many respects, the Captur is a bit shorter than its brother. Featuring an extended overhang, the wagon-like Symbioz offers a bit more luggage space while retaining the Captur's wheelbase.\nRegarding the problem per se, the busbar is the conductive bar that carries current within the battery. A relay creates resistance at the joint, and resistance turns current into heat. Too much of it, and the lithium-ion battery could very well sign off in a ball of fire.\nThe Safety Gate description of the problem reads that abnormal heating of the connection and battery cells may result in a fire, but no such fires have been reported thus far in the European space. Renault 's local subsidiaries will send owners registered letters asking them to book a service appointment.\nPhoto: Renault The fix should take no more than four hours and 30 minutes, traction battery inspection included. Renault is most certainly offering courtesy vehicles during the downtime period. As of this writing, the Symbioz hybrid is sold in France as the E-Tech 160 with 158 mechanical ponies to its name. It's joined by a lower-output gasoline and bi-fuel version with 120 chevaux-vapeur.\nInstead of 29,990 euros for the Eco-G with the manual box, the full-hybrid model retails at 34,400 euros. The Captur sits below it, with prices kicking off at 26,800 euros for the Eco-G and 27,300 euros for the TCe. Upgrading to the noticeably more frugal hybrid means 30,500 euros from the outset.\nCompeting against the Peugeot 2008, Toyota Yaris Cross, and the Volkswagen T-Roc, the Captur is one of the best-selling crossovers in France. It and the Symbioz maintain consistent placement in the country's top 20 best sellers, with Renault being the leading automotive brand in France.\nThe group as a whole experienced a successful year in 2025, driven by strong demand for electrified vehicles in the core brand's home market. Renault alone sold more than a million vehicles across the continent, ranking second overall, while French registrations totaled nearly 285,600 vehicles in 2025. When it comes to best-selling passenger vehicle overall in France, the Renault Clio made easy work of second-placed Peugeot 208 and third-placed Dacia Sandero.\nVia // EC Safety Gate\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nRenault Captur renault SYMBIOZ Renault Hybrid recall\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT WMC Mono RR Ducted Motorcycle Could Be a Sign of Bikes to Come",
